cycle rejects a modulus blind to an axis, which coprimality with the extent does not rule out: true
cycle accepts the shapes the accumulator-width legs use: true
without a radix, cycle computes the hand-written flat idiom it replaces: true
without a radix, cycle refuses modulus 5 on the 20x20 operand: true
a radix coprime to the modulus lifts the blind axis: every index moves the value: true
under that radix the modulus still chooses the value set: exactly the five integers in [-2, 2]: true
a radix off 1 (mod modulus) keeps the square operand distinct from its transpose: true
a radix sharing a factor with the modulus is refused, however it relates to the dims: true
a large radix on a high-rank shape is judged by its residue, not an overflowed power: true
a modulus past Ll_test.max_modulus is refused, and one at it is accepted: true
a radix that would drop a value the row-major key reaches is refused: true
a negative radix mints the operand its residue does: radix -2 is radix 3 under modulus 5: true
under a radix, cycle_flat over the row-major offsets is cycle over the multi-indices: true
weighted computes the hand-written conv-fixture idiom it replaces: true
weighted refuses a weight that is a multiple of the modulus: true
weighted refuses a weight vector of the wrong rank: true
with no weight a multiple of the modulus, weighted varies with every index: true
a negative weight still lands in the value set: true
drift varies with every index of every shape used: true
every drift cell is exact in bf16: true
a drift reduction leaves bf16 exactness within the extents these tests reduce: true
every drift partial sum stays exact in f32: true
the 16-wide row's first bf16-inexact partial sum is the eleventh, at 275/64: true
exact_partials is sound: where it accepts a cell, every partial sum of every association is exact at that width: true
exact_partials accepts some of those cells, and refuses some, at every width: true
exact_partials' bound is tight: it accepts 2^p grains and refuses 2^p + 1, whose sum is inexact at p bits: true
exact_partials refuses mixed signs even where every partial sum is exact (gfx11's WMMA premise): true
exact_partials reads zeros as signless, and an empty or all-zero cell as exact: true
exact_partials refuses a non-finite product: true
exact_partials refuses a width outside 1 .. 52, and takes both ends: true
grain_exponent is the weight of the lowest set bit, and none for zero: true
schedule_mma_matmul's bf16 operands keep every cell exact in bf16: true
their cycle recentred on zero is refused at bf16: true
and it is the sign that refuses it: the magnitudes still fit bf16: true
